<script>
const DATA = [{"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Acne Vulgaris", "summary": "A common inflammatory acne condition affecting pores, oil glands and surrounding skin.", "detail": "Acne vulgaris can appear as blackheads, whiteheads, red bumps, pustules or deeper painful lesions. A dermatologist can assess severity, triggers, skin type and the possibility of scarring before recommending a plan.", "approach": "The approach may include prescription or non-prescription skin care, acne medicines, lifestyle guidance and follow-up depending on severity.", "slug": "acne-vulgaris"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Blackheads", "summary": "Open clogged pores that look dark because of oxidation at the surface.", "detail": "Blackheads are open comedones, not dirt trapped in the skin. Repeated squeezing can irritate skin and increase the chance of marks or inflammation.", "approach": "Care may focus on pore-clearing skin care, comedonal acne treatment and habits that reduce repeated clogging.", "slug": "blackheads"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Whiteheads", "summary": "Closed clogged pores that form small flesh-coloured or white bumps.", "detail": "Whiteheads are closed comedones caused by a mixture of oil and dead skin cells within a pore. They may become inflamed if the blockage persists.", "approach": "A consultation can determine whether topical comedonal acne care or other treatment options are appropriate.", "slug": "whiteheads"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Inflammatory Acne", "summary": "Red, tender or swollen pimples caused by inflammation around blocked follicles.", "detail": "Inflammatory acne can range from small papules and pustules to larger painful lesions. Early assessment matters when acne is persistent or leaving marks.", "approach": "Treatment is selected according to the number, depth and location of lesions and whether scarring is developing.", "slug": "inflammatory-acne"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Cystic Acne", "summary": "Deep, painful acne lesions that can have a higher risk of scarring.", "detail": "Cystic or nodulocystic acne involves deeper inflammation and should not be repeatedly squeezed or picked. It often needs a more structured medical plan.", "approach": "A dermatologist may consider prescription treatment and close follow-up when acne is severe or resistant.", "slug": "cystic-acne"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Hormonal Acne", "summary": "Acne influenced by hormonal changes, often recurring around the lower face or jawline.", "detail": "Hormonal influences can contribute to recurring acne, especially when flares follow a pattern. The full history helps distinguish acne from other follicular or inflammatory conditions.", "approach": "Management depends on the patient, pattern, age, medical history and whether hormonal treatment is appropriate.", "slug": "hormonal-acne"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Back Acne", "summary": "Acne affecting the back, shoulders or upper trunk.", "detail": "Back acne can be worsened by sweating, friction, occlusive clothing and some hair or body products. Lesions may also be harder to reach and treat consistently.", "approach": "Treatment can include body-directed skin care and medical acne treatment chosen according to severity.", "slug": "back-acne"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Chest Acne", "summary": "Acne or acne-like lesions across the chest.", "detail": "Chest breakouts may be true acne, folliculitis or a mixture of causes. A clinical examination helps determine which pattern is present.", "approach": "Care is based on the diagnosis and can include topical or systemic approaches when appropriate.", "slug": "chest-acne"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Acne Marks", "summary": "Flat red, brown or grey marks that remain after acne lesions settle.", "detail": "Post-acne marks are not the same as active acne scars. Their appearance can change gradually, and sun exposure may make some forms more noticeable.", "approach": "A plan usually combines control of new acne with appropriate pigmentation-focused care and sun protection.", "slug": "acne-marks"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Post-inflammatory Hyperpigmentation", "summary": "Darkened patches left behind after inflammation or injury to the skin.", "detail": "Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation can follow acne, rashes, bites or irritation. It is more visible on many deeper skin tones and can take time to fade.", "approach": "Treatment focuses on controlling the original inflammation and may include topical or procedure-based options when suitable.", "slug": "post-inflammatory-hyperpigmentation"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Atrophic Acne Scars", "summary": "Indented acne scars caused by loss of collagen and supporting tissue.", "detail": "Atrophic scars include ice-pick, boxcar and rolling patterns. Different scar types respond differently, so assessment of texture and depth matters.", "approach": "Possible approaches include resurfacing, microneedling, subcision or other scar-focused procedures depending on the scar pattern.", "slug": "atrophic-acne-scars"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Ice Pick Scars", "summary": "Narrow, deep, sharply defined acne scars.", "detail": "Ice-pick scars extend deeper than their visible opening and can be difficult to improve with a single technique.", "approach": "A dermatologist may discuss scar-specific procedures rather than treating all acne scars as if they were identical.", "slug": "ice-pick-scars"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Boxcar Scars", "summary": "Round or oval depressed scars with sharper or softer borders.", "detail": "Boxcar scars can be shallow or deeper and may be more noticeable under certain lighting. The depth and edge of the scar guide procedure selection.", "approach": "Potential options include resurfacing, subcision or combination scar treatment when clinically appropriate.", "slug": "boxcar-scars"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Rolling Acne Scars", "summary": "Wavy or tethered depressions that create an uneven skin surface.", "detail": "Rolling scars often relate to fibrous bands under the skin that pull the surface down.", "approach": "Subcision and combination approaches may be discussed after examination, often alongside other scar procedures.", "slug": "rolling-acne-scars"}, {"category": "Acne & Scars", "title": "Acne Scar Assessment", "summary": "A structured assessment of active acne and the type of scarring left behind.", "detail": "Scar treatment works better when the scar type, skin characteristics and active acne are evaluated separately.", "approach": "The clinic can use the assessment to explain priorities, realistic timelines and possible treatment sequences.", "slug": "acne-scar-assessment"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Melasma", "summary": "Patchy facial pigmentation commonly involving the cheeks, forehead or upper lip.", "detail": "Melasma is a chronic pigmentary condition influenced by multiple factors, including sunlight and hormonal influences.", "approach": "Management commonly emphasizes sun protection and a long-term, individualized pigment-control plan.", "slug": "melasma"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Sun Spots", "summary": "Brown patches that develop after cumulative ultraviolet exposure.", "detail": "Sun spots or solar lentigines can appear as clearly defined pigmented areas on sun-exposed skin.", "approach": "Treatment may include topical care or selected procedures after diagnosis.", "slug": "sun-spots"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Freckles", "summary": "Small, flat pigmented spots that can darken with sun exposure.", "detail": "Freckles are common pigment patterns and may become more visible during periods of higher ultraviolet exposure.", "approach": "The consultation focuses on confirming the pigment pattern and discussing cosmetic options and sun protection.", "slug": "freckles"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Post-acne Pigmentation", "summary": "Brown or grey marks left after inflammatory acne.", "detail": "Pigmentation after acne can persist after the active pimples disappear and is often more noticeable with sun exposure.", "approach": "The plan usually addresses both ongoing acne and the pigment left behind.", "slug": "post-acne-pigmentation"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Dark Neck", "summary": "Uneven darkening of skin around the neck.", "detail": "Dark neck can have several causes, including friction, inflammation, pigmentary conditions or metabolic associations.", "approach": "Evaluation is important before choosing a cosmetic treatment because the underlying cause matters.", "slug": "dark-neck"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Under-eye Pigmentation", "summary": "Darker appearance beneath the eyes with several possible causes.", "detail": "Under-eye darkness may reflect pigmentation, visible vessels, shadows, thin skin or a combination of factors.", "approach": "Treatment options depend strongly on the cause and the sensitivity of the area.", "slug": "under-eye-pigmentation"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Lip Pigmentation", "summary": "Uneven darkening of the lips.", "detail": "Lip pigmentation can be related to genetics, sun exposure, irritation, smoking, medications or inflammatory changes.", "approach": "A history and examination help identify the likely contributors before treatment.", "slug": "lip-pigmentation"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Post-inflammatory Pigmentation", "summary": "Dark spots that develop after skin inflammation.", "detail": "Any inflammatory skin condition can sometimes leave behind temporary or persistent pigmentation.", "approach": "The original condition should be controlled while pigment-directed care is selected carefully.", "slug": "post-inflammatory-pigmentation"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Pigmented Lesion Check", "summary": "Clinical assessment of a changing or unusual pigmented spot.", "detail": "A changing mole or pigmented lesion may need examination rather than cosmetic treatment.", "approach": "Dermatologists may assess shape, colour, borders, symptoms and change over time and decide whether further evaluation is needed.", "slug": "pigmented-lesion-check"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Uneven Skin Tone", "summary": "A general concern involving patchy or inconsistent skin colour.", "detail": "Uneven skin tone may result from pigmentation, inflammation, sun exposure or post-acne changes.", "approach": "A consultation can separate active disease from cosmetic pigment concerns and guide the next step.", "slug": "uneven-skin-tone"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Facial Pigmentation", "summary": "Pigmentation affecting cheeks, forehead, nose or other facial areas.", "detail": "Facial pigmentation can have multiple patterns, and similar-looking patches can have different causes.", "approach": "Diagnosis comes before choosing a treatment plan, especially for persistent or recurrent pigmentation.", "slug": "facial-pigmentation"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Hand Pigmentation", "summary": "Pigmented spots or uneven tone on the hands.", "detail": "Hands receive substantial sun exposure and can develop freckles, lentigines or post-inflammatory pigmentation.", "approach": "Care may include sun protection, topical options or selected procedures when appropriate.", "slug": "hand-pigmentation"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Knee & Elbow Darkening", "summary": "Dark or rough skin over commonly friction-exposed joints.", "detail": "Knees and elbows may appear darker because of friction, dryness, thickened skin or pigmentary changes.", "approach": "Treatment addresses texture and cause rather than simply attempting to bleach the skin.", "slug": "knee-elbow-darkening"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Body Pigmentation", "summary": "Patchy or darker areas on the neck, trunk, arms or legs.", "detail": "Body pigmentation may follow inflammation, friction, infections or other dermatological conditions.", "approach": "The distribution and history help determine what is driving the pigmentation.", "slug": "body-pigmentation"}, {"category": "Pigmentation", "title": "Pigmentation Review", "summary": "Follow-up for monitoring response to pigment treatment.", "detail": "Pigment conditions often improve gradually and can fluctuate with sun exposure or the original trigger.", "approach": "Follow-up helps assess progress, tolerability and whether the plan should be adjusted.", "slug": "pigmentation-review"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Ringworm / Tinea", "summary": "A fungal infection affecting skin, often forming itchy or scaly patches.", "detail": "Tinea can occur on the body, groin, feet or other areas and can spread between people or through shared items.", "approach": "Correct diagnosis matters because several rashes can look similar and antifungal treatment should match the location and extent.", "slug": "ringworm-tinea"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Tinea Corporis", "summary": "Ring-shaped fungal infection of the body skin.", "detail": "Tinea corporis often produces expanding scaly patches with a more active border.", "approach": "Treatment generally focuses on appropriate antifungal therapy and reducing reinfection.", "slug": "tinea-corporis"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Tinea Cruris", "summary": "Fungal infection affecting the groin.", "detail": "Groin fungal infection can be itchy and may spread to adjacent skin.", "approach": "A dermatologist can distinguish it from eczema, intertrigo and other groin rashes before treatment.", "slug": "tinea-cruris"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Athlete’s Foot", "summary": "Fungal infection of the feet and spaces between the toes.", "detail": "Tinea pedis can cause scaling, cracking, itching or maceration between the toes.", "approach": "Care may combine antifungal therapy with foot-drying and hygiene measures.", "slug": "athlete-s-foot"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Nail Fungus", "summary": "Fungal infection that can make nails thick, brittle, yellow or discoloured.", "detail": "Nail changes can have several causes, so examination and sometimes testing can help confirm fungal infection.", "approach": "Treatment depends on the number of nails, severity and medical factors affecting medication choice.", "slug": "nail-fungus"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Dandruff", "summary": "Flaking of the scalp often associated with seborrheic dermatitis.", "detail": "Dandruff can involve fine to greasy flakes and may be accompanied by itching or redness.", "approach": "Management usually focuses on regular scalp care and anti-yeast or anti-inflammatory treatment when indicated.", "slug": "dandruff"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Seborrheic Dermatitis", "summary": "A common inflammatory condition affecting oily areas such as the scalp, eyebrows and around the nose.", "detail": "Seborrheic dermatitis can cause redness, scaling and itching and often follows a recurring pattern.", "approach": "Treatment aims to control inflammation and reduce the factors that trigger recurring flares.", "slug": "seborrheic-dermatitis"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Bacterial Skin Infection", "summary": "Skin infection caused by bacteria, sometimes producing redness, pain, swelling or discharge.", "detail": "Bacterial skin infections vary greatly in depth and severity and can sometimes progress quickly.", "approach": "A clinical examination helps determine whether urgent care, topical treatment or systemic treatment is needed.", "slug": "bacterial-skin-infection"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Impetigo", "summary": "A contagious superficial bacterial infection that often forms crusted sores.", "detail": "Impetigo is more common in children but can affect adults too.", "approach": "Treatment depends on the number and extent of lesions and the clinical diagnosis.", "slug": "impetigo"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Folliculitis", "summary": "Inflammation around hair follicles that can look like small red or pus-filled bumps.", "detail": "Folliculitis may result from bacteria, yeast, friction, shaving or occlusion, so the cause matters.", "approach": "Treatment is directed at the trigger and the specific type of folliculitis.", "slug": "folliculitis"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Scabies", "summary": "An itchy infestation caused by the mite Sarcoptes scabiei.", "detail": "Scabies typically causes intense itching, often worse at night, with characteristic lesions in certain body sites.", "approach": "Treatment usually involves the patient and appropriate close-contact management as advised by a clinician.", "slug": "scabies"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Herpes Zoster", "summary": "A viral infection causing a painful, one-sided blistering rash along a nerve distribution.", "detail": "Shingles can produce pain, grouped blisters and sensitivity in a band-like area.", "approach": "Early medical assessment is important because treatment timing can affect the course and complications.", "slug": "herpes-zoster"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Herpes Simplex Skin Infection", "summary": "Recurring grouped blisters or sores caused by herpes simplex virus.", "detail": "Herpes simplex can recur at the same or nearby sites and may be triggered by illness, stress or other factors.", "approach": "A dermatologist can assess whether antiviral treatment is appropriate and discuss recurrence management.", "slug": "herpes-simplex-skin-infection"}, {"category": "Infections & Inflammation", "title": "Warts", "summary": "Small rough growths caused by human papillomavirus.", "detail": "Warts